  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 AM CDT Fri Jun 26 2026\n\nMost of the CAMs show storms developing on the higher terrain of\nColorado later today. This may lead to a small MCS moving across\nsouthwest Kansas later tonight. Areal coverage of storms is expected\nto be much less in coverage then what we have seen recently. SPC\nhas a risk level 1 out of 5 across western Kansas. So, storms may\nproduce marginal wind and hail reports. Again, this complex is forecast\nto be relatively small per the CAMs, so much of the FA will remain\ndry tonight. Winds will eventually shift SE by evening. These southerly\nwinds and dewpoints running in the 60s will lead to mild lows tonight\nwith values in the 60s, except 70s across south-central Kansas.\n\nThe global models and their ensemble counterparts show fairly high\n500 hPa heights continuing throughout the weekend. This supports\nthe notion of a hot and dry forecast. Highs both days will be in\nthe upper 90sF to around 100F. Lows will trend warmer, and actually\nby Sunday morning, warm lows in the 70s are expected for the FA.\nIn fact, Medicine Lodge may only see a low of around 80F. \n\nLow to mid level moisture advecting around the large anticyclone is\nexpected by the models and their ensemble counterparts Monday and\nTuesday. This could lead to very isolated storms both days. Coverage\nis expected to be limited, so much of the FA will remain just dry\nand hot outside of this limited convection. Highs for the beginning\nof the business week will continue to remain seasonably hot with\nvalues in the upper 90sF to around 100F. Lows will be warm too with\nvalues only bottoming out in the 70s.",
